2️⃣ Retry Pattern 🔄

Retries a failed request a configurable number of times.

Example Use Case:

Calling a payment service

Temporary network glitch occurs

Retry 3 times before failing

Spring Boot Example:
import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Retry(name = "paymentRetry", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public String processPayment(String orderId) {
        // Call remote payment service
        return callPaymentApi(orderId);
    }

    public String fallback(String orderId, Throwable ex) {
        return "Payment service unavailable. Try later.";
    }
}

Configuration (application.yml):

resilience4j.retry:
  instances:
    paymentRetry:
      max-attempts: 3
      wait-duration: 2s

The Retry Pattern automatically retries failed operations, reducing the impact of temporary failures like network glitches or intermittent service downtime.

🔹 How It Works

When a method call fails, Resilience4j retries it a configurable number of times.

You can define:

Max attempts – how many times to retry

Wait duration – delay between retries

Exceptions to retry – only retry certain exceptions

Backoff strategy – e.g., fixed or exponential delays

🔹 Key Benefits

Handles transient failures – like temporary network issues.

Improves reliability – reduces failed requests without complex logic.

Configurable – can retry selectively and avoid overwhelming services.

🔹 Real-World Example 🏦

Scenario: Payment Service calls Fraud Detection API.

Network glitch occurs → API call fails.

Retry Pattern automatically retries the call 3 times before failing.

Optional fallback response ensures user experience isn’t disrupted.

Payment Service → Fraud Detection API
     ↓ Retry 3x → Success / Fallback
🔹 Spring Boot Integration Example
@Bean
public RetryConfig retryConfig() {
    return RetryConfig.custom()
        .maxAttempts(3)
        .waitDuration(Duration.ofSeconds(2))
        .retryExceptions(IOException.class)
        .build();
}

Retry retry = Retry.of("fraudServiceRetry", retryConfig);

Supplier<String> resultSupplier = Retry.decorateSupplier(retry, () -> fraudApi.checkTransaction(txn));
String result = Try.ofSupplier(resultSupplier)
                   .recover(ex -> "Fallback Response")
                   .get();

Retry.decorateSupplier wraps your method with retry logic.

Try.ofSupplier + recover handles fallback responses.

